-- ───────────────────────────── NOTES ─────────────────────────────
-- 1. Application-layer rules (enforce in the Spring Boot service layer,
--    same pattern as account-service and loan-service v1):
--      - A loan can only move to 'ACTIVE' once its DISBURSED stage row
--        in loan_origination_stages is COMPLETED and a matching row
--        exists in disbursements with status = 'COMPLETED'.
--      - A loan can only move to 'REJECTED' if its latest
--        credit_assessments.assessment_result = 'REJECTED'.
--      - emi_schedule rows should be generated in a batch job right
--        after disbursement, not materialized upfront for the full
--        tenure (e.g. don't pre-create all 240 rows for a 20-year home
--        loan at disbursement time) — generate a rolling window (e.g.
--        next 12 months) and top it up monthly instead.
-- 2. A collections row should only exist for emi_schedule rows where
--    payment_status IN ('OVERDUE','MISSED') — enforce this in the
--    collections-worker job, not the DB (Postgres CHECK constraints
--    can't reference another table's row state).
-- 3. grade / risk_grade is derived from credit_score — recompute it in
--    application code on every new assessment rather than trusting a
--    stale stored value.
-- 4. loans.updated_at should be bumped by the app (or a trigger) on
--    every status change, stage completion, or new assessment — this
--    script only seeds the initial value.
-- 5. Sample data is synthetic, generated to resemble realistic loan
--    origination/servicing patterns — not real applicant or transaction
--    data.
